use ../lib/CodingRule

from ./CertC import CertC

export coding-rule CertCInt36C:
    standard: @{CertC}
    ruleId: INT36-C
    title:
        Convert between pointers and integers only in well-defined ways
    statement:
        Do not convert an integer to a pointer unless the result is properly
        aligned and points to an object of the referenced type, and do not
        convert a pointer to an integer type too small to hold it. Use
        intptr_t or uintptr_t when such a conversion is needed.
    rationale:
        These conversions are implementation-defined, and a pointer that
        does not fit its integer type is undefined behavior, so code breaks
        when pointer sizes change. CERT rates likelihood probable,
        detectable yes, repairable no, priority P4, level L3.
    exceptions:
        The constant zero turns into the null pointer and is always safe. A
        void pointer that points to a real object can make a round trip
        through intptr_t or uintptr_t. An integer that fits may likewise
        travel through a void pointer, provided nobody dereferences it on
        the way.
    severity: low
    noncompliant:
        ```c
        \\\
        unsigned int handle_of(void *obj) {
            return (unsigned int)obj;
        }
        \\\
        ```
    compliant:
        ```c
        \\\
        #include <stdint.h>

        uintptr_t handle_of(void *obj) {
            return (uintptr_t)obj;
        }
        \\\
        ```
    languages: [c]
    tags: [integers, pointers, conversions]
    source: https://cmu-sei.github.io/secure-coding-standards/sei-cert-c-coding-standard/rules/integers-int/int36-c
